  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sat Apr 18 2026\n\n...SUNDAY...\nContinued cold air advection behind yesterday's cold front will \nkeep the region cool on Sunday. Lowland highs will only reach the \nlower 70s, 5-10 degrees below normal for mid-April. More notable \nwill be the gusty winds early Sunday morning and through the \ndaytime hours. 1027mb surface high over Oklahoma/Texas will\ntighten surface pressure gradients, reinforcing east winds across\nSouthwest New Mexico. Wouldn't be surprised to see wind gusts in \nthe 30 to 40 mph range across the New Mexico Bootheel and\nFranklin/Organ/Florida Mountain west slopes. Areas of blowing \ndust are possible near the Lordsburg Playa along I-10 Sunday \nafternoon.\n\nMoisture return up the Rio Grande valley will increase dewpoints\nand push precipitable water back up to around 0.8\" Sunday night.\nThis will lead to more cloud coverage and a very slim (10-20%)\nchance of rain showers Sunday night into Monday. This is mainly\nfor El Paso/Hudspeth Counties. Any rain that does fall will be \nvery light (<0.05\").\n\n...MONDAY AND TUESDAY...\nWarming trend early next week, with lowland highs back into the \nmid 80s by Tuesday afternoon. Ridging flow aloft will keep wind \nspeeds down over this timeframe, so minimal weather threats. \nMarginal moisture sticks around on Monday, which could lead to \nisolated showers and thunderstorms over the high terrain \n(Sacramento Mountains, Black Range, Hudspeth Highlands) otherwise \nthe rest of the region will be dry. \n\n...WEDNESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY... \nStrengthening southwest flow ahead of a Pacific low will bring \nmore breezy to windy conditions midweek. Right now, deterministic \nmodels show a Pacific low swinging across the Central Rockies well\nto our north thus no precipitation chances. Thursday currently \nlooks like the strongest winds, though below Advisory criteria. \nConfidence is high in a warm, dry, and breezy weather pattern the \nrest of next week.\n\n...NEXT WEEKEND...\nBenign weather in the extended forecast. Low-end breezy wind each\nafternoon, warm late April temperatures, and continued dry \nconditions.",
